<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarikh al-Sudan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1655</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tarikh al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> is a chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> written in Arabic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> by Abd al-Rahman al-Sadi and completed around 1655. It records the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, the Moroccan conquest of 1591 and the rule of the pashas, as well as the lives of the scholars of Timbuktu and Djenne. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-fattash">Tarikh al-Fattash</a>, it is the most important local source for the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger bend</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan.jpg" alt="Tarikh al-Sudan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: إيان · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AE_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The author, Abderrahmane al-Sa'di, was born on 28 May 1594 and died at an unknown date after 1655-56, the last date his chronicle mentions. He spent most of his life working for the Moroccan Arma bureaucracy, first in the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a> region of the Inland Niger Delta, and from 1646 as chief secretary to the Arma administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. Together with the Tarikh al-fattash, another 17th-century history of Songhay, his work is known as one of the Timbuktu Chronicles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The opening sections give short histories of earlier Songhay dynasties, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>, and biographies of the scholars and holy men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>. The main part covers the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> from the middle of the 15th century to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroccan-invasion-of-songhai">Moroccan invasion of 1591</a>, and then Timbuktu under Moroccan rule. Al-Sa'di seldom names his sources; for the earlier period he presumably relied on oral tradition, and from around 1610 he wrote from first-hand knowledge. He also recorded the tradition that a ruler named Kaya-Magha founded the first kingdom in the region, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and that forty-four kings reigned before the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> took over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1853 the German scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> for the British government, consulted a copy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwandu-emirate">Gwandu</a> in present-day northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, but believed the author to be the Timbuktu scholar Ahmed Baba. Forty years later the French journalist Félix Dubois, in Timbuctoo the Mysterious, pointed out that the chronicle records Ahmed Baba's death and so could not be his work.</p><p>After the French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> in the 1890s, the Bibliothèque Nationale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> acquired two copies: one sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-archinard">Louis Archinard</a> and one made for Dubois at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> in 1895. The Arabic scholar Octave Houdas studied these and a third, better copy dated 1792, sent by the linguist René Basset, and published the Arabic text in 1898 and a French translation in 1900. A century later John Hunwick published a partial English translation and listed four further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, which differ mainly in the spelling of names.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tarikh al-Sudan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Written in the seventeenth century, the great Timbuktu chronicles, the <b>Tarikh al-Sudan</b> and Tarikh al-Fattash, preserve the memory of Songhai itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest document to mention it is Abd al-Sadi's <b>Tarikh al-Sudan</b>, which gives the early history, presumably from the oral tradition as it existed in the mid-seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-mosque-of-djenne">Great Mosque of Djenné · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Visitors described a fertile, crowded land, and the <b>Tarikh al-Sudan</b>, written in the 17th century, claimed that the villages around Djenné stood so close that a message could be shouted from one to the next.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Timbuktu remained a centre of scholarship, and local historians wrote the <b>Tarikh al-Sudan</b> and other chronicles in this period.</blockquote><a href="/state/pashalik-of-timbuktu">Pashalik of Timbuktu</a></li></ul></section>
